so welcome to the to today's a  conference for today and tomorrow the  conflict in the South China Sea  my name is Eric harms I'm an associate  professor of anthropology and a member  of the Council on Southeast Asian  Studies here at Yale as a Vietnam  specialist I'm especially pleased to be  hosting this conference here because as  so many of you know the conflict in the  South China Sea what the Vietnamese  called the eastern sea being dumb is  currently the most central issue facing  the country you will forgive me for  exercising a bit of Vietnam Oh centrism  I hope but for the rest of my remarks  and throughout the conference  I will refer to the body of water at the  center of our conference as the eastern  sea everyone is free to call it whatever  they would like today and tomorrow but  that is what I will call it we are in  for a series of important contributions  to this topic in the first panel we will  have a series of experts commenting on  the history of the dispute in the  eastern sea where we will learn about  how various maps have been deployed to  argue over sovereignty in the panel  after that we will have papers on the  geopolitics of the sea focusing on why  so many nations want to stake claims to  it and the various machinations they  employ in trying to do so and tomorrow  we will have a series of papers on what  we are calling law fair the use and  abuse of international law to contest  claims in the region throughout these  proceedings we have experts joining us  from around the world among the 14  papers counted 14 being presented  we have guests from the United Kingdom  Germany Canada France Australia the  Philippines and Vietnam as well as  us-based scholars coming from Washington  DC Maine California Boston and Rhode  Island as moderators and discussants we  have members of the Connecticut Vietnam  studies group coming from nearly all the  cardinal points of the Connecticut State  University system with representatives  from Southern Connecticut State Eastern  Connecticut state and Central  Connecticut State our friend from  Western Connecticut State is attending a  cousin's graduation out-of-state we also  have discussants and moderators from  Dartmouth College and new hand  from New York State as well as different  Yale departments across the university  we have some Vietnamese news media  present as well and we are also filming  all of these events which will  ultimately be posted to Yale's YouTube  channel and also made available to  interested new news organizations so  think about that as you're making your  comments and presentations indeed there  has been so much interest in the topic  that we had to have required  registration for seating at the event  given the small size of this room  but I want to stress stress that this is  not an attempt to censor the the form of  discussion in any any way and we look  forward to a very wide diversity of  opinions on the matters at hand is  merely a result of logistical  considerations especially the size of  the room so thank you for your  understanding I have a few of my own  remarks about this topic that I'll make  in just a moment but before I do that I  want to take a moment just to thank all  the many parties who've been involved in  organizing this event first and foremost  is to thank mr. Dunning son who is the  mastermind of this wonderful project  please he's a Fulbright Scholar visiting  us at Yale this semester we've been very  fortunate to benefit from his wisdom in  many presentations and talks and of  course assembling this wonderful group  of people today we also have a support  from mr. Kang John who's in the back was  from the and Hank Gunn should be known  to many of you as a the organizer and  mastermind of the IVC II the Institute  for Vietnamese culture and education and  he's been doing a lot of wonderful  programming about Vietnamese events not  just here but in in many occasions also  a Quan Fubon please stand up Quang who  is  a Vietnamese teacher philosopher  literate literature scholar who's also  been working extremely hard on this  project and most importantly I want to  thank miss Christine música in the back  from the Caliphate Southeast Asian  Studies please stand up Chris Chris took  on this event in addition to all of her  her normal everyday duties which are  quite many and so it's a real testament  to her dedication to the Southeast Asian  Studies program at Yale for all the work  she did an entire las' efforts to make  this happen and I want to thank Michael  Dov as well the chair of the Council of  Southeast Asian Studies for agreeing to  bring this into our year's program so  now bear with me for about 10 minutes or  so I have a few of some comments that  I'd like to make given my background as  an anthropologist who's been working in  Vietnam for close to 20 years the first  time I went to Vietnam was in 1997 and  so given the many approaches to the  topic about to be presented in this  conference which mainly focus on  politics history and law I thought it  might be useful as an anthropologist to  step back and give a sense of how  sensitive this topic is to Vietnamese in  in a more everyday perspective and to  use this as a way to convey some of the  emotions and controversies at hand to do  so I was thinking about how to get how  to start back and I want to step back in  time about two years almost to the same  time of the year two years ago on May  2nd 2014 when China play state-owned oil  rig HD 981 inside Viet Nam's exclusive  economic zone 120 nautical miles off the  coast of Vietnam not far from the  Paracel Islands which along with the  Spratly Islands further south had long  been the focus of sino-vietnamese  maritime disputes as we all know these  waters are claimed by both China and  Vietnam as well as other nations in the  region and Carl Thayer who's with us  today in an article on the topic at the  time accurately remarked at the time  that China's act was quote unexpected  provocative an illegal end quote and  most well-informed international  observers generally agree  with that assessment the placement of  the rig led almost immediately to  protests across Vietnam even the  Vietnamese government which had until  then been extraordinarily cautious in  its dealings with China formally and  forcefully demanded that the rig be  withdrawn a Vietnamese naval flotilla  was deployed to the area but the oil rig  was protected by as many as 80 ships  including seven People's Liberation Army  Navy warships and on May 4th Vietnamese  Coast Guard vessels were sprayed with  cannons and rammed by Chinese ships in  the days that followed the protests in  the streets of Vietnamese cities became  increasingly boisterous and anti-chinese  sentiment in Vietnam became increasingly  volatile by May 12th reports in the  Chinese media claimed that protesters  had directed their anger on the Chinese  embassy in Hanoi and protests were also  growing in cities like Ho Chi Minh City  and Danang as well as other parts of the  country Chinese media at the time tended  to portray these acts as being tacitly  sanctioned by the Vietnamese government  within Vietnam however the tone was  different many Vietnamese bloggers and  everyday citizens were instead insisting  that the leadership in Hanoi was acting  too timid in the face of China in  Vietnam popular anger against China's  action continued to seethe on Tuesday  May 13th rioting broke out in industrial  parks in Binion province near Ho Chi  Minh City and the violence continued  into Wednesday May 14th the violence  itself had followed protests that were  estimated in the range of 20,000 people  mostly workers from the industrial parks  by May 15th  hundreds of Chinese had fled the border  to Cambodia and even more deadly  violence erupted that same day in north  central Vietnam at a Taiwanese steel  mill in the bang boom hang economic zone  in Ha Tinh province where a crowd of  1000 workers killed six Vietnamese and  16 others described as Chinese while the  urban street protests had begun with  expressions of nearly united national  sentiment in protest of the  Chinese actions the tenor of the  discourse shifted when news began to  emerge about the violence against  Chinese people and businesses many  everyday citizens were especially  dismayed to hear that many of the  so-called Chinese people and businesses  being attacked were in many cases  actually Taiwanese Singaporean or even  Korean nationals by Saturday May 17th  Vietnamese officials signaled that  further violence over a dispute with  China would not be tolerated declaring  that over 300 people involved in the  attacks on foreign owned businesses  would be prosecuted the issue was so  important that then Prime Minister and  when Tania conveyed the edict to the  people through a text message  although anti-chinese sentiment remained  and to this day still remains an  important force throughout the general  population all of a sudden the  Vietnamese government realized that it  was facing a confrontation not only with  China but also in some ways that it was  being forced into an uneasy relationship  with its own people I opened this  conference with this reminder about the  events of 2014 for several reasons the  obvious one is to show how volatile this  situation is and to remind us of the  stakes involved those events in 2014  were not isolated that was not the first  time that the geopolitical waters of the  sea have grown rough in the summer of  2009 Chinese navy boats impounded  multiple Vietnamese fishing vessels  leading to similar volatility on the  streets but not the explicit violence  and for the past couple of years we've  been learning of report after report  about new Chinese constructions on the  islands which keep the mood on the  streets quite tense  although most observers like to analyze  the eastern sea issue primarily in terms  of global geopolitics I wish to  emphasize that the issue isn't also  inflected by the complex internal  politics within countries themselves the  question is not just about which country  has sovereignty over the eastern sea but  also how governments in the region  established their own legitimacy with  their own people since around  and seven as bill hatin has written in  his book various incidents have  regularly brought crowds to the streets  of Vietnamese cities and the government  is in the delicate position  of either surfing the crest of that  nationalist wave or being overtaken by  it on the other hand on the one hand the  party state also has the delicate task  of trying to control the unpredictable  human forces that can become unleashed  during moments of extraordinary  ethno-nationalism put simply the problem  of the eastern sea has the potential to  either enhance or threaten the  legitimacy of the Vietnamese government  an optimist might say that this is  actually an opportunity for the  Vietnamese government to reconcile a lot  of interests in the country and to bring  people together it's one of the few  cases for example if you look around at  the audience here today where Vietnamese  Americans and Vietnamese in Vietnam are  united in the interest in this topic and  you can see this as an opportunity for  the Vietnamese government to start  thinking about potential ways to unify  people but a cynic will question whether  the leaders have the political will to  be trustworthy stewards of national  sentiment whatever direction the  Vietnamese government decides to go it  is clear that the Vietnamese people are  watching and the trust of the people  needs to be earned in recent weeks as  China has been continuing with its  constructions of various islands in the  middle of the eastern sea the Vietnamese  people have been concerned about a  different aspect of the sea the ocean  environment and this time they are  directing their anger not to China alone  but also looking internally to what they  perceive as a non-transparent set of  relationships between foreign industries  and Viet Nam's leaders after the recent  discovery of massive fish deaths in the  eastern sea suspicions emerged that the  deaths were caused by a large-scale  dumping of pollution from a steel print  plant owned by Formosa Plastics while  the government has not offered  conclusive connections between the  company and the fish deaths popular  opinion holds it responsible and has  decried the lack of information being  made available the slogan on signs  across the country reads gakuen young  comin back fish need water people need  transparency it's with that call for  transparency in mind that this  conference precedes governments would  their real politic and their sensitive  diplomatic protocols are often forced to  tread lightly or dance around the truth  or to carefully manage what they do or  do not share as academics we have a  different task and that is to lay it all  out on the table and to present the  facts as best we can and it's with that  spirit that I hope to open this  conference thank you so now I'd like to  introduce Michael dub who's the chair of  the Council of Southeast Asian Studies  who also has some opening remarks I'm  quite tall I have problems with low  lecterns so I may just speak from here  with Eric I'd like to welcome you to  this conference thank you for this  opportunity to hear your insights on the  conflict in the South China Sea like  Eric I'm an anthropologist but I work  elsewhere in the region and I thought I  would offer a view of this conflict from  the region some of our panelists will  note the importance of taking an  historical perspective to this conflict  there's a long history in the region of  the transformative impact of  technological innovation in conflict  situations a much storied example were  the 17th century Dutch warships whose  technology of synchronized firing was  without parallel and region and brought  low the powerful naval citadel of  makasar  in 1667 such direct exertions of  military might can overshadow however  more indirect enactments of power which  may have equal if not greater impacts  think of the historic development in the  region of irrigated rice terraces recent  research suggests that rice and  irrigation do not actually greatly  benefit individual households but they  do support the growth of state  structures we might term such  innovations terraforming the the term  originally refers to the process of  transforming a planet into one  sufficiently similar to the earth to  support terrestrial life we could use it  to refer to the process of transforming  an environment like that's the South  China Sea into one more conducive to  state expansion the transformation of  large tracts of land in the region into  export crop plantations during the  colonial era is another such example as  is also the contemporary conversion of  even greater tracts of land to oil palm  plantations such schemes entail shifts  not simply in plant cover but in 10  Uriel rights regimes of knowledge and  political sovereignty but the biggest  impact of terraforming may lie in the  way that it code develops with issues  like those mentioned by professor harms  earlier issues of cultural identity  civilizational ideals and the perceived  arc of history terraforming like the  dredge  in the South China Sea is also history  forming it's an attempt to alter not  just the physical landscape but the  historical landscape and with that I  believe we proceed to photograph
 

